    {
      "id": 1,
      "text": "Normalville Church, founded in 1849 by German immigrants, joined the United Methodist Church in 1968 and still hosts food banks and volunteer programs.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/normalville_pennsylvania"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The community first settled in the late 1700s after the Revolutionary War, originally called Springfield village in Springfield Township.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/normalville_pennsylvania"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "In the 1800s Normalville grew amid Fayette County’s coal and iron industry, but today it emphasizes community and conservation.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/normalville_pennsylvania"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Winters in Normalville often bring heavy snowfall from the Appalachians, while humid summers can bring thunderstorms.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        7
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/normalville_pennsylvania"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Normalville sits along state routes 381 and 711, just 7.6 miles east of Connellsville.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        9
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Normalville%2C_Pennsylvania"
    },
    {
